rgistic effect sort of uh  this holistic approach to using cannabis  flower versus say  extracted oil or you know a more refined  product so if you go back and look at  like a lot of  uh canvas flower competitions the thc  levels among the winners  typically are right around the same as  the flowers that don't make it into the  winning categories but the terpene  levels tend to be  uh very diverse and abundant compared to  uh you know the the winners typically  have this very  sort of abundant terpene profile versus  the uh  the strings that don't work  so you know when when cannabis science  first started to really evolve there was  so much focus  on cannabinoids and i think largely  there still is and for good reason  um but over the last few years there's  been  this sort of shift towards you know  larger interest in terpenes  and the terpene profiles of strains  and you know like i was talking about  earlier is such a  key piece to the whole sensory  experience of consuming cannabis  i think that people really are becoming  more and more curious  as to uh you know what unique and  interesting  terpene profiles these strings have and  you know they can vary so significantly  from  from one string to another so  i think that there's a lot of traction  that is starting to build  specifically with terpene analysis  so when you make the decision that you  want to analyze terpenes in your lab  um you know unless you're using an  out-of-the-box method which there's  nothing wrong with that  but i find personally that a lot of  times i  like to sort of uh adjust these methods  a little bit to more  suit my needs so if you're starting from  the ground up  it's very important to sort of identify  some uh key ideas and concepts around  your method before you really begin  method development  this can save you significant time down  the road  particularly if you're using headspace  so it's worthwhile in my opinion to kind  of  take a step back before you start this  method 