ood starting  point there's a number of  uh commercial mixed standards out there  that  contain 22 terpenes so that's usually  where i advise people to start  particularly with the commercial  laboratory  once you've made this decision then you  can move on to your column selection  and this is important because  here you really need to identify the  goals of your method  and a few considerations for this is the  number of samples that you plan on  running per day  and also a lot of labs particularly if  you're on a tight budget  use the same instrument to run residual  solvents and  terpenes which can cut your  analysis time you know  down quite a bit uh you know there's  also combination methods we can run  residual solvents with terpenes and so  on and so forth  um but if you're utilizing  the single instrument for both residual  solvent and terpene  analysis you most likely want to have a  faster method say under 15 minutes  and then you have to think about your  desired detection limits uh  you know how far down you want to look  uh there's a big difference between  a very sort of robust and practical  method versus  one that gets very very granular and  finally you know are you is this an  academic research lab  is it a commercial research lab or you  know  commercial compliance lab these are all  very important questions to identify  and with that also you have to look at  sort of the samples that you're going to  be running  you know i've worked in cannabis labs  all over the country  and you know the types of samples that  are received  are very regional so in northern  california typically  laboratories tend to be a lot more  flower focused southern california  while there is still of course flower  you see a lot of more products  uh you know we tested dog treats and  feline transdermal patches  and pretty much anything you can think  of to put cannabis in  uh you know we've seen come across at  some point  so it's important to understand this too  is ther