atories to do that  the primary disadvantages of uh liquid  injection are that  you know all the volatile and  non-volatile compounds are injected  in the gc which you know with your  your liner can uh cause it to become  dirty pretty quickly  so if you're working in sort of a high  throughput lab  it's difficult to sort of run you know  50 or 100 samples  in an automated manner with liquid  injection  because there is you know quite a bit  more maintenance than  headspace  but one of the you know and then  headspace one of the great advantages of  headspace is depending on where  you are in the country you know certain  states have minimum  required masses for analysis uh  you know i probably wouldn't put more  than 40 or 60 milligrams  into a headspace file to analyze it  directly  you know the idea of headspace is that  you  either analyze you know there's one of  well there's multiple ways that you can  do this but  for interpreting analysis really you do  a total evaporative technique  or direct analysis and so you can just  put the sample directly in there  and you know cook it for 15 minutes and  inject it and it's a pretty effective  technique it's used  widely for uh flour analysis for  trippings and  flowers and flower research now  the problem with direct analysis is  depending on your matrix type you may  run into some  matrix issues oils particularly i did an  entire study on  recoveries from oils  with known concentrations and direct  analysis particularly for the heavy  boilers so i'll put this below  things sort of towards the end of the  list you can run into a lot of matrix  type issues there the other real  big advantage of it is that it's an  ultra clean technique  so the amount of day-to-day maintenance  and you know week-to-week maintenance is  absolutely minimal um when i was working  for brickenhammer  i uh ran into a customer that had been  using the same filament  i think for four years which is pretty  impressive  so it just goes to show you how how  clean this technique is and  you know you get a lot more lifespan out  of your column and so on and so forth  uh one of the biggest disadvantages  really to headspace is that  method development is mind-numbingly  painful  because you have to wait for the vial to  heat up so say  you have a 15-minute incubation time  then you're waiting for that 15 minutes  plus another 15-minute run time  so you're looking at 32 minutes plus  from injection to injection but once the  methods developed you really only pay  that price  first you know with the first sample  most modern headspace systems  uh have staged um  incubation so that's not as much as a  concern as it  was years ago  once you've decided on your injection  technique you want to look at  whether you're going to use a split so  split flipless injections  i think basically all modern gc's for  the most part  um have split surface injection ports  so a split basically allows  for you to only inject a fraction of the  effluent into the actual column  and the the remainder is sort of swept  off  and vented so this is useful  primarily in uh liquid injection  so there's you know you don't want to  overload the column  so depending on what you're doing you  can go anywhere from like a ten to one  split to 100 to one split  uh depends on your sample prep how  concentrated your injection is and so on  and so forth  um footless injections primarily used  with headspace but you can do a split  if you want typically what i do is i'll  adjust the actual injection time on the  headspace unit  setting splits and uh you know basically  when you run into  this injection um the uh this football  just remains closed  so the next phase of this is sorting out  your chromatography  and the separation of terpenes  can be kind of difficult and the primary  reason for that  is that because they are these sort of  five units or  sorry five carbon repeating units of  isoprene um they are structurally very  similar  and uh you k